questionvariables.text:
/* Row records: [id, correct name, likely wrong name, another wrong name, claim, argument, explanation (shown on a correct answer and in the worked solution), lead (shown when the likely wrong name is chosen: why that name does not fit, without naming the right one)]. */
r_ok1: ["ok1", fl_ok, fl_cases, fl_zero, "the sum of any two odd numbers is even.", "Write the two numbers as \\(2j + 1\\) and \\(2k + 1\\), where \\(j\\) and \\(k\\) are integers. Their sum is \\(2j + 2k + 2 = 2(j + k + 1)\\), which is even.", "This is a proof. Using two different letters, \\(j\\) and \\(k\\), covers every pair of odd numbers, and each step is correct.", "For that name to fit, some pair of odd numbers would have to be left out. Try to find one: for a pair such as 3 and 11, or 7 and 7, can you choose \\(j\\) and \\(k\\) to fit?"];
r_ok2: ["ok2", fl_ok, fl_claim, fl_zero, "\\(n^2 - n\\) is even for every integer \\(n\\).", "\\(n^2 - n = n(n - 1)\\) is the product of two consecutive integers. One of any two consecutive integers is even, so the product is even.", "This is a proof. The factorisation is correct, and the fact it uses, that one of any two consecutive integers is even, is true.", "For that name to fit, the argument would have to rely on a general statement that is not true. The one it relies on is that one of any two consecutive integers is even. Test it, with negative integers and 0 as well: can you make it fail?"];
r_ok3: ["ok3", fl_ok, fl_circ, fl_zero, "\\(x^2 - 6x + 10 \\gt 0\\) for every real number \\(x\\).", "\\(x^2 - 6x + 10 = (x - 3)^2 + 1\\). Since \\((x - 3)^2 \\geq 0\\), it follows that \\(x^2 - 6x + 10 \\geq 1 \\gt 0\\).", "This is a proof. It starts from a known fact, \\((x - 3)^2 \\geq 0\\), not from the claim, and every step is correct.", "For that name to fit, the argument would have to use \\(x^2 - 6x + 10 \\gt 0\\) as a step. Look at what it uses instead: is \\((x - 3)^2 \\geq 0\\) the claim, or a fact that is known without it?"];
r_ok4: ["ok4", fl_ok, fl_conv, fl_zero, "if an integer \\(n\\) is odd, then \\(n^2\\) is odd.", "Write \\(n = 2k + 1\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer. Then \\(n^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1\\), which is odd.", "This is a proof. It starts from the hypothesis, \\(n\\) is odd, and reaches the conclusion, \\(n^2\\) is odd, by correct algebra.", "For that name to fit, the argument would have to start from ‘\\(n^2\\) is odd’ and reach ‘\\(n\\) is odd’. Look at where it starts and where it ends."];
r_zero1: ["zero1", fl_zero, fl_ok, fl_slip, "if \\(x^2 = 5x\\), then \\(x = 5\\).", "Dividing both sides of \\(x^2 = 5x\\) by \\(x\\) gives \\(x = 5\\).", "This argument divides by zero: \\(x\\) can be 0, and \\(x = 0\\) satisfies \\(x^2 = 5x\\). So the claim is false.", "If this were a proof, the claim would be true. Test it: find every value of \\(x\\) with \\(x^2 = 5x\\), and see whether each of them is 5. If the claim is false, some step of the argument must fail."];
r_zero2: ["zero2", fl_zero, fl_ok, fl_slip, "if \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are real numbers with \\(ab = ac\\), then \\(b = c\\).", "Dividing both sides of \\(ab = ac\\) by \\(a\\) gives \\(b = c\\).", "This argument divides by zero: \\(a\\) can be 0. With \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = 1\\) and \\(c = 2\\), \\(ab = ac\\) but \\(b \\neq c\\).", "If this were a proof, the claim would be true. Test it: can you find real numbers with \\(ab = ac\\) but \\(b \\neq c\\)? If you can, some step of the argument must fail."];
r_slip1: ["slip1", fl_slip, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\((n + 2)^2 - n^2\\) is a multiple of 4 for every integer \\(n\\).", "\\((n + 2)^2 - n^2 = n^2 + 4 - n^2 = 4\\), which is a multiple of 4.", "This argument makes an algebra slip: \\((n + 2)^2 = n^2 + 4n + 4\\), not \\(n^2 + 4\\). The claim is true, since \\((n + 2)^2 - n^2 = 4(n + 1)\\), but this argument does not prove it.", "In a proof, every line can be checked. Check this one with a number: for \\(n = 1\\), work out \\((n + 2)^2 - n^2\\), and compare it with what the argument says it equals."];
r_slip2: ["slip2", fl_slip, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\((x + 1)^2 \\geq x^2\\) for every real number \\(x\\).", "\\((x + 1)^2 = x^2 + 1\\), which is at least \\(x^2\\).", "This argument makes an algebra slip: \\((x + 1)^2 = x^2 + 2x + 1\\), not \\(x^2 + 1\\). In fact the claim is false: \\(x = -3\\) gives \\(4 \\lt 9\\).", "In a proof, every line can be checked. Check this one with a number: for \\(x = 2\\), work out \\((x + 1)^2\\), and compare it with what the argument says it equals."];
r_claim1: ["claim1", fl_claim, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\(\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{8}\\) is irrational.", "\\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{8}\\) are both irrational, and the product of two irrational numbers is always irrational.", "This argument uses a false claim: the product of two irrational numbers can be rational. Here \\(\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{8} = \\sqrt{16} = 4\\), so the claim is false too.", "If this were a proof, the claim would be true. Test it: work out \\(\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{8}\\) exactly. If the claim is false, something in the argument must fail."];
r_claim2: ["claim2", fl_claim, fl_ok, fl_zero, "if \\(p\\) is a prime number, then \\(p^2 + 2\\) is odd.", "Every prime number is odd, so \\(p^2\\) is odd, and so \\(p^2 + 2\\) is odd.", "This argument uses a false claim: not every prime is odd. For \\(p = 2\\), \\(p^2 + 2 = 6\\), which is even.", "If this were a proof, the claim would be true. Test it with the first few prime numbers, starting with the smallest. If the claim fails for one of them, something in the argument must fail."];
r_claim3: ["claim3", fl_claim, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\(2\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational.", "\\(2\\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}\\), and the sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational.", "This argument uses a false claim: \\(\\sqrt{2} + \\left(-\\sqrt{2}\\right) = 0\\) is a sum of two irrational numbers that is rational. The statement that \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational is true, but it needs a correct reason.", "The statement is true, but that does not make the argument a proof: everything the argument relies on must be true as well. Check each thing it relies on, with examples of your own."];
r_circ1: ["circ1", fl_circ, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\(x^2 + 4x + 5 \\gt 0\\) for every real number \\(x\\).", "Since \\(x^2 + 4x + 5 \\gt 0\\), subtracting 1 from both sides gives \\((x + 2)^2 \\gt -1\\), which is true. So \\(x^2 + 4x + 5 \\gt 0\\).", "This argument assumes what it should prove: it starts from the inequality it is trying to prove. A proof starts from what is known: \\(x^2 + 4x + 5 = (x + 2)^2 + 1 \\geq 1 \\gt 0\\).", "In a proof, every line rests on what is given or on what has already been shown. Look at the first words of the argument, ‘Since \\(x^2 + 4x + 5 \\gt 0\\)’: what does that rest on?"];
r_circ2: ["circ2", fl_circ, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\((x + 1)^2 - (x - 1)^2 = 4x\\) for every real number \\(x\\).", "Assume that \\((x + 1)^2 - (x - 1)^2 = 4x\\). Expanding the brackets gives \\(4x = 4x\\), which is true, so the identity holds.", "This argument assumes what it should prove: it starts from the identity and simplifies it. A proof works on the left-hand side alone: \\((x + 1)^2 - (x - 1)^2 = (x^2 + 2x + 1) - (x^2 - 2x + 1) = 4x\\).", "In a proof, every line rests on what is given or on what has already been shown. Look at the first sentence of the argument: what does it assume, and what is the argument meant to show?"];
r_circ3: ["circ3", fl_circ, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\(3^n - 1\\) is even for every positive integer \\(n\\).", "We know that \\(3^n - 1\\) is even for every \\(n\\). Then \\(3^{n+1} - 1 = 3(3^n - 1) + 2\\) is even as well, so \\(3^n - 1\\) is even for every \\(n\\).", "This argument assumes what it should prove: its first sentence is the claim itself. A proof by induction assumes the statement for one value, \\(n = k\\), and also checks \\(n = 1\\).", "In a proof, every line rests on what is given or on what has already been shown. Look at the first sentence of the argument: how do ‘we know’ it?"];
r_conv1: ["conv1", fl_conv, fl_ok, fl_zero, "for every integer \\(n\\), if \\(n^2\\) is a multiple of 3, then \\(n\\) is a multiple of 3.", "Let \\(n = 3k\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer. Then \\(n^2 = 9k^2 = 3(3k^2)\\), which is a multiple of 3.", "This argument proves the converse: it starts from the conclusion, \\(n\\) is a multiple of 3, and shows that \\(n^2\\) is a multiple of 3. The claim itself is true, but it needs a different proof, for example by cases on the remainder of \\(n\\) on division by 3.", "Every step is correct, but ask what the steps prove, taken together. The claim has the form ‘if P, then Q’. Which of P and Q does the argument start from, and which does it reach?"];
r_conv2: ["conv2", fl_conv, fl_ok, fl_zero, "for real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), if \\(xy = 0\\), then \\(x = 0\\) or \\(y = 0\\).", "If \\(x = 0\\), then \\(xy = 0 \\times y = 0\\). If \\(y = 0\\), then \\(xy = x \\times 0 = 0\\).", "This argument proves the converse: it starts from the conclusion, \\(x = 0\\) or \\(y = 0\\), and shows that \\(xy = 0\\). The claim is true, but this argument does not prove it.", "Every step is correct, but ask what the steps prove, taken together. The claim has the form ‘if P, then Q’. Which of P and Q does the argument start from, and which does it reach?"];
r_cases1: ["cases1", fl_cases, fl_ok, fl_zero, "\\(n^2 + n\\) is even for every positive integer \\(n\\).", "\\(1^2 + 1 = 2\\), \\(2^2 + 2 = 6\\), \\(3^2 + 3 = 12\\) and \\(4^2 + 4 = 20\\), which are all even.", "This argument covers only some cases: four values of \\(n\\) out of infinitely many. The claim is true, since \\(n^2 + n = n(n + 1)\\) is a product of two consecutive integers, but checking examples does not prove it.", "Every calculation is correct, but ask what the calculations prove, taken together. The claim is about every positive integer \\(n\\). For which values of \\(n\\) does the argument show it?"];
r_cases2: ["cases2", fl_cases, fl_ok, fl_zero, "the sum of any two odd numbers is even.", "Let the two numbers be \\(2k + 1\\) and \\(2k + 1\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer. Their sum is \\(4k + 2 = 2(2k + 1)\\), which is even.", "This argument covers only some cases: using the same letter \\(k\\) twice makes the two numbers equal, so it covers only pairs such as 5 and 5. Different letters, \\(2j + 1\\) and \\(2k + 1\\), cover every pair.", "Every step is correct, but ask what the steps prove, taken together. Try to fit a pair of odd numbers to the argument, for example 3 and 7: which value of \\(k\\) gives both?"];
r_cases3: ["cases3", fl_cases, fl_ok, fl_zero, "the sum of any three consecutive integers is a multiple of 3.", "Write the integers as \\(3k\\), \\(3k + 1\\) and \\(3k + 2\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer. Their sum is \\(9k + 3 = 3(3k + 1)\\), a multiple of 3.", "This argument covers only some cases: it covers runs that start with a multiple of 3, such as 6, 7, 8, but not 7, 8, 9. Writing the integers as \\(n\\), \\(n + 1\\) and \\(n + 2\\) covers every run: their sum is \\(3(n + 1)\\).", "Every step is correct, but ask what the steps prove, taken together. Try to fit a run of three consecutive integers to the argument, for example 7, 8, 9: which integer \\(k\\) gives \\(3k = 7\\)?"];
vlist: [[r_zero1, r_ok1, r_conv1, r_cases3],
  [r_claim1, r_circ2, r_ok3, r_slip1],
  [r_cases1, r_zero2, r_claim2, r_ok4],
  [r_conv2, r_slip2, r_ok2, r_circ3],
  [r_circ1, r_cases2, r_zero1, r_claim3],
  [r_ok1, r_slip1, r_conv2, r_claim2],
  [r_ok4, r_circ2, r_cases3, r_zero2],
  [r_slip2, r_conv1, r_ok3, r_cases1]];
vrows: rand(vlist);
cor1: vrows[1][2]; diag1: vrows[1][3]; oth1: vrows[1][4];
cor2: vrows[2][2]; diag2: vrows[2][3]; oth2: vrows[2][4];
cor3: vrows[3][2]; diag3: vrows[3][3]; oth3: vrows[3][4];
cor4: vrows[4][2]; diag4: vrows[4][3]; oth4: vrows[4][4];
fbcor1: sconcat("Yes. ", vrows[1][7]);
fbdiag1: vrows[1][8];
fbcor2: sconcat("Yes. ", vrows[2][7]);
fbdiag2: vrows[2][8];
fbcor3: sconcat("Yes. ", vrows[3][7]);
fbdiag3: vrows[3][8];
fbcor4: sconcat("Yes. ", vrows[4][7]);
fbdiag4: vrows[4][8];
/* Any other wrong name: what the chosen name would need, in the words of the box, without naming the right one. */
nudge(c) := if c = fl_zero then "‘Divides by zero’ fits only if a step divides both sides by something that is 0, or might be 0. Is there a division in this argument? If there is, can the divisor be 0?" else if c = fl_slip then "‘Makes an algebra slip’ fits only if there is a mistake in expanding, simplifying or taking square roots. Check each such step in this argument, with a number if it helps. Is any of them wrong?" else if c = fl_claim then "‘Uses a false claim’ fits only if the argument relies on a general statement that is not true. Find each general statement that this argument relies on, and test it with examples. Is any of them false?" else if c = fl_circ then "‘Assumes what it should prove’ fits only if the whole statement being proved is used as a step. Compare the claim with each line of this argument. Is the claim itself used?" else if c = fl_conv then "‘Proves the converse’ fits only if the claim has the form ‘if P, then Q’ and the argument starts from Q and reaches P. Does this claim have that form? If it does, where does the argument start, and where does it end?" else if c = fl_cases then "‘Covers only some cases’ fits only if the argument checks examples, or writes the objects with letters in a way that fits only some of them. Does this argument do either?" else "‘Has no flaw’ fits only if every line follows and the lines, taken together, prove the claim as stated. Test the claim with examples, check each step, and ask where the argument starts and what it covers.";
mkopts(c) := [[fl_zero, is(c = fl_zero), "divides by zero"], [fl_slip, is(c = fl_slip), "makes an algebra slip"], [fl_claim, is(c = fl_claim), "uses a false claim"], [fl_circ, is(c = fl_circ), "assumes what it should prove"], [fl_conv, is(c = fl_conv), "proves the converse"], [fl_cases, is(c = fl_cases), "covers only some cases"], [fl_ok, is(c = fl_ok), "has no flaw: it is a proof"]];
ta1: mkopts(cor1); ta2: mkopts(cor2); ta3: mkopts(cor3); ta4: mkopts(cor4);
s_assert(every(lambda([i], is(vrows[i][2] # vrows[i][3]) and is(vrows[i][2] # vrows[i][4]) and is(vrows[i][3] # vrows[i][4])), [1, 2, 3, 4]), true);
